  <h2>What makes a waiver conditional</h2>
  <p>
    Every lien waiver trades a right for a payment. The only question is when the trade happens. A
    conditional waiver says the release becomes effective when you have received the payment stated
    on it and the check has been endorsed and paid by the bank. Until then it releases nothing. That
    makes it safe to sign in advance, and it is why owners and general contractors accept it with a
    pay application: they have your signed release in hand, and you keep your rights if the money
    does not come.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its counterpart is the <a href="/templates/unconditional-lien-waiver/"
      >unconditional lien waiver</a
    >, effective on signing. The <a href="/templates/lien-waiver/">four-form lien waiver set</a> has both
    kinds together; this page has the two conditional forms on their own. For how the two kinds trade
    places over a billing cycle, and the wording to push back on, read
    <a href="/blog/construction-lien-waivers-explained/"
      >conditional vs. unconditional lien waivers explained</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Progress or final</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Conditional progress (form 1)</th><th>Conditional final (form 2)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Covers</strong></td><td>One payment, for work through the through date</td><td
          >The whole job, retention included</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Keeps</strong></td><td
          >Retention, pending change orders, disputed items, later work</td
        ><td>Only the disputed claims you list</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Send with</strong></td><td>Each progress application or invoice</td><td
          >The final application and closeout package</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Statutory-form states</h2>
  <p>
    A general form is only as good as the state allows. At least twelve states — California, Texas,
    Arizona, Nevada, Utah, Georgia, Florida, Massachusetts, Michigan, Missouri, Mississippi and
    Wyoming — prescribe waiver wording by statute; the
    <a href="/templates/lien-waiver/">lien waiver forms page</a> lists the statute for each. Florida’s
    forms are in § 713.20(4) and (5), and § 713.20(6) bars anyone from requiring a different form. In
    those states, use the statutory form; do not assume a generic conditional waiver will hold.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example follows the pay application sample. Application no. 3 bills August 2026 on
    a mechanical subcontract, with a current payment due of $122,557.50. The conditional progress
    waiver names the general contractor as customer and the developer as owner, uses a through date
    of August 31, 2026 and a payment amount of $122,557.50, and carves out $28,680.50 of retention
    and a $5,784.50 change order submitted but not yet approved. It is dated September 3, with the
    application. The conditional final waiver goes with the closeout application through November
    30, which releases the $49,735.45 of retention on the adjusted contract.
  </p>

  <h2>The exchange, step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Submit the <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application</a> or invoice with a conditional
      progress waiver for the current payment due.
    </li>
    <li>
      When that payment clears, sign the matching
      <a href="/templates/unconditional-lien-waiver/">unconditional progress waiver</a> if the GC or the
      owner’s lender asks for one — often it travels with the next month’s application.
    </li>
    <li>
      Collect the same conditional waivers from your own sub-subcontractors and suppliers before you
      pay them; most subcontracts make your payment depend on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      At closeout, send the conditional final waiver with the final application and the closeout
      documents, and sign the unconditional final only after the retention has cleared.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    On a Florida job, the subs and suppliers who served a
    <a href="/templates/notice-to-owner/">notice to owner</a> (after the owner recorded a
    <a href="/templates/notice-of-commencement/">notice of commencement</a>) are the ones the owner
    will want waivers from before each payment — and in Florida those waivers follow the § 713.20
    statutory forms.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Using a general form in a statutory-form state.</li>
    <li>A payment amount equal to the gross billed instead of the current payment due.</li>
    <li>Blank exceptions, so retention and pending changes look released.</li>
    <li>Signing an unconditional waiver when the GC asked for a conditional one, or vice versa.</li>
    <li>Not collecting matching conditional waivers from your own suppliers and sub-subs.</li>
  </ul>
</TemplatePage>
